{"text":"Recent work supports separating transient intent from persistent preferences and learning their relative contributions. [LSTASRec (2025 preprint)](https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=5238143) combines independent long/short-term extractors, temporal modeling, and adaptive gated fusion. [Multi-Scale Transformers (2026)](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ipm.2025.104318) uses dual attention for transient and stable interests, with adaptive masking to reduce redundant interactions. [HeteroTempRec (2026)](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ins.2025.122703) combines local convolution, global attention, temporal fusion, and selection of relevant historical blocks. These provide architectural precedents; their reported results do not establish gains on this pinned Amazon benchmark.

For sparse histories, [LS4SRec (2025)](https://doi.org/10.1145/3723173) supplements separate interest encoders with collaborative signals from an interaction graph. [Elastic Multi-intent Adaptation (2024)](https://doi.org/10.1109/TKDE.2024.3354796) explicitly retains existing intents, detects emerging ones, and removes inactive representations, although its incremental-learning setting differs from fixed-split training. Neither approach requires treating observed purchases as satisfaction labels.

A lightweight adaptation worth testing: retain SASRec, add a recent-window pooling branch and a history-wide attention-pooling branch, then learn a gate conditioned on their representations, observed history length, and timestamp gaps. This is a proposed simplification, not an established result from these papers. Use causal masking at every training position; compare against fixed fusion and each branch alone using macro validation HR@10. Treat latent intents as predictive representations, not measured user traits. Because evaluation excludes seen items, repeated interests should inform unseen-item ranking rather than directly reward repurchases."}
